<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
    Layer catalog: additional sounds that can be attached to any motorized vehicle (dialog tab "Extra sounds": one row per
    group, saved per vehicle model). Each <layer> holds one sample definition in the vehicle sound XML format (<motor ...>),
    loaded with g_soundManager:loadSampleFromXML(handle, "layers.layer(i)", "motor", ...) and added to the vehicle's
    motorSamples, so Motorized starts / stops it with the motor and updates rpm / load (tested 2026-10-01).

    name      unique id, also the l10n key msc_layer_<name> (translated since v0.16.0.0); never rename (saved in the settings)
    group     dialog group, l10n msc_group_<group> and msc_help_group_<group>; groups are shown in the order
              of their first layer, max. 9 groups (dialog rows); one layer per group can be active at a time.
              Every layer of the group "engine" is also a button on the "Engine swap" tab (src/EngineSwap.lua)
    shortName button text on the "Engine swap" tab (group "engine" only; brand names, not translated, sorted by it)
    category  motor | gearbox | cabin | rattle: which category factors apply (except volume, layers have their own) (default: from the template / file name)
    source    vehicle the sound comes from, shown in the help line (brand names, not translated)
    baseGameFile  instead of source for base game sounds without a source vehicle: file / template name, shown as
              l10n msc_layer_baseGame ("base game (%s)" / "Basisspiel (%s)")
    needsSuspension  true: the sound uses the SUSPENSION modifier, attached only to vehicles with spec_suspensions

    source="own": no sound file, clones the vehicle's own main engine sample (g_soundManager:cloneSample) with
    pitchFactor / rpmFactor / volumeFactor relative to its original values; modifiers inside <motor> are added to
    the clone (SoundManager:loadModifiersFromXML).

    Files point at base game data ($data/...). .gls files follow the rpm by loop synthesis, .ogg / .wav loops need
    pitch / volume modifiers (MOTOR_RPM_REAL = rpm, MOTOR_LOAD = load 0..1, as in the base game vehicles).
    Volume calibration v0.13.0.0 (hearing test of the user 2026-10-02): 100 % = about as loud as the original engine of
    a vehicle. The engine volumeScales were about half of the source vehicles' own values (JD 3650 0.6 vs 1.2, MF 8570
    1.1 vs 2.2), the user heard the original loudness at 200 % (300 % for Deutz Series 7, Fendt 200, Kubota M8 and the
    own engine octave / fifth), so these were doubled / tripled; the load versions of the old engines likewise.
    Exhausts Xerion (Load), Pm Trac and Pm Trac (Load) doubled as well (second hearing test, 200 % = new 100 %).
    Third hearing test (new 100 % = old value): CLAAS Arion 200 %, JD 7R / Valtra T / MF 8570 / Zetor 150 %,
    Fiat 90 (and its load version) 90 %, Transtar II 70 %.
    Fourth hearing test (engine swap, user 2026-10-13, JD 3650, swapped engine vs. own engine with "Hear original";
    new 100 % = old value x the slider value that sounded equally loud): Quadtrac, Vestrum, NH TK4, Forterra, Proxima 95 %,
    Xerion, Deutz Series 7, Kubota M8, Landini, McCormick, MF 5700 S, MF 9S, Zetor Crystal 110 %, Deutz 6C,
    Deutz Series 8, Valtra S 120 %, Fendt 200 115 %, AgroStar, Fendt MT 1100, MF 7S, MF 8570 80 %, Fiat 90, JD 9RX 830,
    Transtar II, Valtra T 90 %, Rigitrac 70 %, all others 100 % (load versions unchanged).
-->
